{
  "index": 9,
  "response": " To find the x-intercept, we set y equal to zero because the x-intercept is the point where the graph intersects the x-axis (y=0).\n\nSo, for the equation y = 2x + 4, setting y equals to zero gives:\n\n0 = 2x + 4\n\nNow subtract 4 from both sides of the equation:\n\n-4 = 2x\n\nFinally, divide both sides by 2:\n\nx = -2\n\nSo, the x-intercept is (-2, 0). The point (-2, 0) means that when x equals -2, y (or the value on the y-axis) is 0. This is where the graph intersects the x-axis.",
